      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Row 77. Hardiness: RHS H4 (-10 to -5 C), NC State has no zone line for the UK; this is the least hardy of the batch. Toxicity: RHS says the fruit are ornamental and not to be eaten and to wear gloves when handling, with no named animal, severity or symptom; ASPCA letter pages for V and L were fetched and list no Viburnum tinus entry (page 1 of 4 only for L), NC State has no toxicity line. So toxicity stays unknown (pets unknown, never pet-safe). Watering: RHS guide says to water newly planted viburnums as needed for the first year or two, especially in dry periods, and has no potted-plant section; NC State says drought tolerant and good drainage. let_top_dry and 7 days are the agent's mapping and a guess. Light and sun: RHS profile full sun, partial shade and full shade, guide most viburnums like an open sunny site, NC State full sun to partial shade; bright_indirect and part are the agent's reading of that spread. Size: RHS 2.5 to 4 m both ways (to 3 m tall), NC State 6 to 12 ft by 6 to 10 ft; 3 m is the agent's pick. Placement: pages describe a border shrub; pot_outdoor is the starter-list row, not a statement of a source. Missouri Botanical Garden failed twice (certificate error). Fetched through a summarising fetch: wording not checked against the raw pages. Es and it names are machine drafts (laurentino, lentaggine are the names I know people use; not sourced)."
      },
